  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Customer Context**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the user\'s description. It seems HQ central may not be the customer. I\'m trying to differentiate between the HQ entity, the owner of the application (who manages everything), the operational system of the registered store, and the end customer, who consumes the public catalog and marketplace. I\'m currently parsing the roles and their relationship to one another.\n\n\n**Clarifying Key Relationships**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the nuances of the user\'s statement. The emphasis is on separating \"HQ Central\" from the actual customer base. I\'m visualizing the architecture: HQ Central belongs to the SaaS owner, managing everything like tenants, plans, the MDM fleet, mobile apps, treasury, regional portals, the global CRM, and infrastructure. It\'s becoming clearer where the central management lies, distinct from the end-users.\n\n\n**Defining the System Components**\n\nNow, I\'m delineating the Operating System (OS) for each registered store. This is the core system, not to be confused with a central HQ system or the end-customer interface. It encompasses the internal operations: point-of-sale, table management, kitchen display, inventory, cash management, logistics, and store-level administration. I\'m focusing on clarifying how each store\'s OS is independent and tailored to its operational needs.\n\n\n**Isolating User Access**\n\nI\'m now separating the final customer\'s perspective. They interact solely with public-facing pages: regional marketplaces with geo-location, store catalogs with menus, and order tracking. Critically, there\'s *no* access to the store\'s Operating System, nor the central HQ. I\'m noting the clear delineation between what end-users can see and access. I\'m paying close attention to `/os` route, and the components related.\n\n\n**Refining OS Terminology**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the correct terminology for the store\'s Operating System. My focus is on eliminating ambiguity. I have located conflicting uses of the word \"client\" versus \"customer\", which has led to confusion regarding the correct term. The fix is to use \"STORE OS\" in English, and its Portuguese equivalent. I am also reviewing component naming, such as `ClientOsComponent`, to ensure this aligns with the established distinctions between the HQ system and the store level.\n\n\n"
