    thinking: "**Analyzing the Context**\n\nI\'m digging into the specifics of the user\'s stress, carefully dissecting the meaning behind the phrases. I\'m focusing on identifying the core issues within the user\'s statements regarding HQ, clients, and operational systems. The goal is a precise and thorough understanding.\n\n\n**Defining the Roles**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the distinct roles and responsibilities within the system. My focus is on ensuring a clear separation between the \"HQ Central\" functionalities, which I see as the exclusive domain of the SaaS Owner, and the \"OS da Empresa\" belonging to the registered businesses. The user\'s input clearly highlights the need for this distinction, so I am incorporating this information.\n\n\n**Clarifying System Boundaries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the system\'s external interfaces, primarily the public-facing pages for the end consumer. Specifically, I\'m delineating the \"Marketplace Regional\" and the individual \"Store Catalog & Showcase\" views. I\'m ensuring there\'s a strict boundary between these consumer-facing elements and the operational systems used by the store itself. My attention is now on the codebase, specifically checking `/src/app/components/client-os.ts`.\n\n\n**Differentiating Ownership Clearly**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the code to check for consistent terminology. The findings reinforce a clear separation of ownership. \"OS DA EMPRESA\" (Store OS) is consistently linked to the registered businesses, while \"HQ Central\" remains the SaaS owner\'s domain. The \"Client OS\" ambiguity is now replaced with \"Store OS\" in key areas, as shown by the changes. This clarifies the architecture considerably.\n\n\n"
