    thinking: "**Analyzing User Feedback**\n\nI\'ve reviewed BUG-021 and BUG-023 within `BUGS_AND_SOLUTIONS.md` as a starting point. Now, I\'m diving into the user\'s specific feedback, trying to grasp the implications of the \"o hq central ele nao deve estar o cliente os\" statement and other clues. I\'m focusing on the relationship between the HQ, the client, and the app\'s owner to identify the core issue here.\n\n\n**Defining System Scope**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the system\'s precise scope based on this new information. Specifically, I\'m defining the roles and capabilities associated with the HQ Central. This includes detailing the owner\'s role in the SaaS environment, managing registered companies, handling financial splits, defining pricing plans, and overseeing the platform\'s logistics and mobile apps, including the Driver App\'s APK/AAB audit. I\'m focusing on \"o \303\251 da empresa loja cadrastradas, o cliente final consumidor paginas publicas o catalogo e market place\".\n\n\n**Clarifying Component Boundaries**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on precisely what *doesn\'t* belong within the HQ Central. It\'s becoming clearer that the \"Store OS,\" or the \"cliente os\", functions outside of the HQ\'s domain. The HQ isn\'t a point-of-sale system, it\'s the global command center. Further, I\'m examining how this relates to components such as the Marketplace App, Comanda de Gar\303\247om, Vitrine do Lojista, Portal Regional, CRM, and the infrastructural implications. I am now refining the boundaries of each.\n\n\n**Delineating Local System Components**\n\nI\'m now focusing on specific modules and their functionalities within the Store OS. I\'m clarifying that the consumer *doesn\'t* interact directly with the Store OS, which includes the components: barcode, scale, cash drawer, fiscal document issuance, table management, kitchen display, inventory control, local DRE, logistics hub, store director management, and store manager settings. My analysis now zeroes in on user access levels for each.\n\n\n**Defining Public-Facing Areas**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the public-facing areas. I\'m focusing on the Marketplace Regional (`/regional`), which leverages geodesic GPS for discovery, filtering, and delivery information. The Catalog/On-Demand Showcase (`/p/:id`) is also being refined as the online menu with features like categories, photos, and transparent checkout. I\'m also confirming the system\'s tracking functionality. Importantly, the consumer\'s experience is now clearly separated from the management interfaces.\n\n\n**Revising System Roles**\n\nI\'m now refining the system roles based on user interactions. \"Store OS\" is clearly for the registered store, acting as the PDV. Public-facing areas are now categorized for consumers, like the Marketplace and catalog. The roles now include Driver, and Waiter apps. I am now updating and labeling these components.\n\n\n\n**Refining Role Definitions**\n\nI\'m now revising the role definitions based on the new components and interactions. I\'ve re-categorized \"Public Pages\" to \"Marketplace Regional & Online Catalogs,\" the Driver role as \"Delivery Routes & Geodesic GPS,\" and the Waiter role as \"Table Orders & Table Service.\" I am also adding new architecture badges in `hq-central.ts`. I am also removing confusion from buttons in the HQ. Finally, in `HQCentralComponent`, I\'m looking at the tenants, plans and devices subtabs.\n\n\n**Defining HQ Central Modules**\n\nI\'m now identifying and defining the modules in HQ Central more precisely. I\'m focusing on the `apps_preview`, the `ledger` which is SaaS Treasury, the `regional` Portal, the `consumers` CRM, the `system` Infrastructure, and the `audit` Master Audit. These modules, I\'ve confirmed, are solely for the App Owner\'s management and control of the entire platform, including POS terminals and Driver integration. I am also clarifying the \"Store OS\" labeling for clarity in `client-os.ts`.\n\n\n"
